1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,741
Your CTO has a calm question: "A contractor
says two days of work vanished after she

2
00:00:09,781 --> 00:00:12,947
'checked out a tag to test
something'. Is it gone?"

3
00:00:13,287 --> 00:00:16,455
She checked out a tag, a
fixed name for one commit.

4
00:00:16,495 --> 00:00:21,612
She kept working, and committed, for two
days. Then she switched back to main, and

5
00:00:21,652 --> 00:00:24,882
her commits aren't in any
log she knows how to read.

6
00:00:24,922 --> 00:00:26,769
Gone, or not? Say it out loud.

7
00:00:31,609 --> 00:00:36,298
No, it's not gone. The commits exist, and
are recoverable from the reflog, Git's

8
00:00:36,338 --> 00:00:41,146
local diary of where HEAD has been. And
Git told her so at the moment she left: it

9
00:00:41,186 --> 00:00:45,994
printed a warning, named the commits, and
printed the command that would have kept

10
00:00:46,034 --> 00:00:50,959
them. This video is about the state she was
in, why it's normal, and why one command

11
00:00:50,999 --> 00:00:54,329
is enough to keep work made
there. Remember that warning.

12
00:00:54,369 --> 00:00:56,457
You'll read it yourself in the demo.

13
00:00:58,297 --> 00:01:02,122
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:01:02,162 --> 00:01:07,063
"Detached HEAD" is one of the most searched
phrases in Git, and it sounds like

15
00:01:07,103 --> 00:01:12,322
damage. It's not an error. HEAD is Git's
note of where you are, and video 22 showed

16
00:01:12,362 --> 00:01:17,454
its three possible contents. This is the
second: a commit ID in place of a branch

17
00:01:17,494 --> 00:01:17,770
name.

18
00:01:18,110 --> 00:01:22,551
Today you enter the state on purpose,
commit in it, leave it, lose sight of the

19
00:01:22,591 --> 00:01:27,032
commits, and bring them back. Then you
see the ordinary operations that put you

20
00:01:27,072 --> 00:01:31,400
there without asking: checking out a tag
or a remote-tracking branch, bisect,

21
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,463
rebase, a clone of one release,
a worktree from a tag.

22
00:01:34,503 --> 00:01:38,660
If some of those words are new, that's
fine. Each has its own video later.

23
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:42,078
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:42,418 --> 00:01:43,831
After this video you can:

25
00:01:44,171 --> 00:01:48,760
Explain detached HEAD as HEAD holding
a commit ID in place of a branch name.

26
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:51,667
List the ordinary operations
that detach HEAD.

27
00:01:52,007 --> 00:01:55,302
Make commits while detached,
and keep them before leaving.

28
00:01:55,642 --> 00:01:58,267
Recover commits left behind
after switching away.

29
00:02:00,107 --> 00:02:04,955
In one sentence. HEAD is detached when
it holds a commit ID instead of a branch

30
00:02:04,995 --> 00:02:09,719
name. Git works normally, and new commits
advance HEAD itself rather than any

31
00:02:09,759 --> 00:02:13,246
branch. Here HEAD sits on the
tagged commit, not on main.

32
00:02:13,586 --> 00:02:18,122
Precisely. The manual says that in detached
HEAD state "HEAD refers to a specific

33
00:02:18,162 --> 00:02:22,868
commit, as opposed to referring to a named
branch", and that a commit made there "is

34
00:02:22,908 --> 00:02:27,444
referenced only by HEAD". Git detaches
HEAD whenever you ask for a commit that no

35
00:02:27,484 --> 00:02:30,212
branch names, and several
commands do it for you.

36
00:02:30,552 --> 00:02:36,156
Recall the state table from video 19. git
commit in detached HEAD sets HEAD to the

37
00:02:36,196 --> 00:02:41,869
new commit ID. There is no current branch,
no branch moves, and one line is written

38
00:02:41,909 --> 00:02:43,108
in logs/HEAD only.

39
00:02:43,448 --> 00:02:49,060
Quick quiz. You made these two commits
while detached, and now you switch to main.

40
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:53,609
What happens to them? A, Git deletes
them. B, they move onto main.

41
00:02:53,649 --> 00:02:56,366
C, they stay, with no name. Your answer?

42
00:03:01,206 --> 00:03:06,010
What happens when you leave. The answer
is C. Commits made while detached are

43
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:10,916
reachable only through HEAD. Once HEAD
moves to a branch, no ref reaches them.

44
00:03:10,956 --> 00:03:15,509
They still exist as objects, and the
reflog of HEAD still refers to them.

45
00:03:15,849 --> 00:03:21,335
Three ways to ask "which branch am I on".
Each answers differently when the answer

46
00:03:21,375 --> 00:03:25,042
is "none". git symbolic-ref
HEAD fails with status 128.

47
00:03:25,082 --> 00:03:30,703
git branch --show-current prints nothing.
git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD prints the

48
00:03:30,743 --> 00:03:34,006
literal word HEAD. Scripts
must handle all three.

49
00:03:34,346 --> 00:03:40,033
Where git status gets its words. It reads
the HEAD reflog backwards for the latest

50
00:03:40,073 --> 00:03:45,761
checkout: moving from ... to X entry. X is
printed after "at" if HEAD still equals

51
00:03:45,801 --> 00:03:51,488
that entry's commit, and after "from"
otherwise. With no such entry, as in a fresh

52
00:03:51,528 --> 00:03:57,006
clone, it prints Not currently on any
branch. The textbook cites the Git 2.55.0

53
00:03:57,046 --> 00:03:58,124
source for this.

54
00:03:58,464 --> 00:03:59,702
Every way it happens.

55
00:04:00,042 --> 00:04:04,856
Checking out a tag. Checking out a commit
by ID, or by a relative name such as

56
00:04:04,896 --> 00:04:08,964
HEAD~1. Checking out a remote-tracking
branch such as origin/main.

57
00:04:09,004 --> 00:04:14,006
git bisect, a binary search over commits:
every step checks out a commit to test.

58
00:04:14,046 --> 00:04:19,047
git rebase, while it runs or is stopped.
The rebase replays commits on a detached

59
00:04:19,087 --> 00:04:24,151
HEAD, and moves the branch only at the end.
That's why an interrupted rebase looks

60
00:04:24,191 --> 00:04:26,143
like a detached HEAD: it is one.

61
00:04:26,483 --> 00:04:31,713
git clone --branch <tag>: a clone of one
release, the shape of many deploy scripts.

62
00:04:31,753 --> 00:04:37,048
git worktree add with a tag or a commit.
git submodule update: the default mode puts

63
00:04:37,088 --> 00:04:41,112
each submodule on a detached HEAD,
and that one is not run here.

64
00:04:41,152 --> 00:04:44,922
And a CI checkout, the copy an
automated build job works in.

65
00:04:45,262 --> 00:04:50,448
Keeping the work. Three commands create
a ref for the commit HEAD is on, as the

66
00:04:50,488 --> 00:04:55,476
manual lists them. git switch -c <name>
attaches HEAD to a new branch there.

67
00:04:55,516 --> 00:04:59,578
git branch <name> creates the
branch and leaves HEAD detached.

68
00:04:59,618 --> 00:05:04,077
git tag <name> creates a tag. All
three are 🟢 SAFE: each adds a ref.

69
00:05:04,117 --> 00:05:07,848
After you've moved away, find
the ID in git reflog first.

70
00:05:08,188 --> 00:05:12,810
When to use it, and when not. Use it on
purpose to look at an old state, to test a

71
00:05:12,850 --> 00:05:17,415
release, to try an experiment you may throw
away. Don't leave it with commits you

72
00:05:17,455 --> 00:05:22,077
want and no name for them. The rule for
people is one sentence: before you leave a

73
00:05:22,117 --> 00:05:25,090
detached HEAD with commits
on it, give them a branch.

74
00:05:25,430 --> 00:05:30,861
How long recovery works. The reflog is the
safety net, and it expires: by default,

75
00:05:30,901 --> 00:05:36,265
entries not reachable from the current tip
after 30 days, as you learned in video

76
00:05:36,305 --> 00:05:39,401
21. The lab configuration
sets expiry to never.

77
00:05:41,241 --> 00:05:46,097
A picture helps. The textbook's analogy:
reading a book with no bookmark in it.

78
00:05:46,137 --> 00:05:49,630
You can read any page, and
even write notes on new pages.

79
00:05:49,670 --> 00:05:53,782
But when you put the book down,
nothing marks where your notes are.

80
00:05:54,122 --> 00:05:58,958
The analogy breaks because Git keeps a
diary of where you've been, the reflog, and

81
00:05:58,998 --> 00:06:01,337
the notes can be found again through it.

82
00:06:01,677 --> 00:06:05,431
So the contractor's two days
are notes on unmarked pages.

83
00:06:05,471 --> 00:06:07,228
The diary says which pages.

84
00:06:07,568 --> 00:06:11,436
One bookmark, placed now, makes
them part of the story again.

85
00:06:13,276 --> 00:06:18,263
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
three commits in a row, and write main

86
00:06:18,303 --> 00:06:23,291
under the last one. Then draw HEAD twice:
once attached, and once detached on the

87
00:06:23,331 --> 00:06:27,139
middle commit. What does each
arrow point at? Say it out loud.

88
00:06:31,979 --> 00:06:37,057
Attached first. HEAD points at a branch,
and the branch points at a commit.

89
00:06:37,097 --> 00:06:37,671
Two hops.

90
00:06:38,011 --> 00:06:42,587
Now detached, two commits later.
HEAD points at a commit directly.

91
00:06:42,627 --> 00:06:48,252
One hop. Two commits grow from the tagged
commit 8c6d240, and the only thing that

92
00:06:48,292 --> 00:06:54,057
names the newer one, b23bce3, is HEAD. There
is no branch label on that lower line.

93
00:06:54,097 --> 00:06:57,414
If HEAD moves away, the
line has no name at all.

94
00:06:59,254 --> 00:07:04,949
Into the lab. Check out a tag. The script
uses the older git checkout spelling here,

95
00:07:04,989 --> 00:07:10,206
which detaches without being asked. git
switch --detach is the explicit form.

96
00:07:10,246 --> 00:07:11,299
Both are 🟢 SAFE.

97
00:07:14,639 --> 00:07:20,708
Read the advice block. It's Git's own
explanation, printed once per detach unless

98
00:07:20,748 --> 00:07:26,290
advice.detachedHead is false. Then the
evidence. .git/HEAD holds a raw ID.

99
00:07:26,330 --> 00:07:32,625
git status says HEAD detached at v0.1.0.
And git branch lists a pseudo-entry instead

100
00:07:32,665 --> 00:07:34,134
of a current branch.

101
00:07:34,474 --> 00:07:39,591
The three answers to "which branch am I
on": an error with status 128, an empty

102
00:07:39,631 --> 00:07:41,353
line, and the literal HEAD.

103
00:07:41,693 --> 00:07:45,793
Now two commits. Predict: which
ref moves? Say it out loud.

104
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:57,286
No branch. The summary line says [detached
HEAD f678c98], then [detached HEAD

105
00:07:57,326 --> 00:08:02,588
b23bce3]. The status line now
says HEAD detached from v0.1.0.

106
00:08:02,628 --> 00:08:09,020
The word "at" became "from", because
HEAD has moved since it was detached.

107
00:08:09,060 --> 00:08:15,017
And git log --all shows the new
commits, because --all includes HEAD.

108
00:08:15,357 --> 00:08:20,712
Now leave. Before the output: what do you
expect Git to print, and will git log

109
00:08:20,752 --> 00:08:25,219
--all still show the two commits
afterwards? Make your prediction.

110
00:08:30,059 --> 00:08:35,700
Read the warning aloud. "You are leaving
2 commits behind, not connected to any of

111
00:08:35,740 --> 00:08:39,233
your branches." It names
them: b23bce3 and f678c98.

112
00:08:39,273 --> 00:08:44,844
And it prints the command that keeps them:
git branch, a new branch name, and the

113
00:08:44,884 --> 00:08:50,595
ID. That's the warning the contractor was
given. Then git log --all no longer shows

114
00:08:50,635 --> 00:08:52,396
them: no ref reaches them.

115
00:08:52,736 --> 00:08:56,348
git branch experiment/judge-threshold
b23bce3 🟢 SAFE.

116
00:08:56,388 --> 00:09:01,860
The graph shows both commits again. One
command. And if the warning has scrolled

117
00:09:01,900 --> 00:09:07,373
away, git reflog has the ID: look for the
commit: lines above the last checkout:

118
00:09:07,413 --> 00:09:09,440
moving from an ID to a branch.

119
00:09:09,780 --> 00:09:15,175
Two more ways in. git switch --detach
origin/main: a remote-tracking branch isn't a

120
00:09:15,215 --> 00:09:20,545
local branch, so standing on it means
detached. And git bisect, which checks out a

121
00:09:20,585 --> 00:09:21,527
commit to test.

122
00:09:24,867 --> 00:09:30,925
During a stopped rebase, git branch prints
(no branch, rebasing main), and .git/HEAD

123
00:09:30,965 --> 00:09:34,627
holds an ID. The rebase
moves main only at the end.

124
00:09:37,467 --> 00:09:39,099
By relative name and by ID.

125
00:09:39,439 --> 00:09:43,637
A deploy script that clones one
release with --branch v0.1.0.

126
00:09:43,677 --> 00:09:48,917
The clone has no local branch at all.
git status prints Not currently on any

127
00:09:48,957 --> 00:09:53,780
branch., because the fresh clone's
HEAD reflog has no checkout: entry.

128
00:09:53,820 --> 00:09:59,546
A deploy script that commits a generated
file in such a clone commits onto nothing.

129
00:09:59,886 --> 00:10:04,736
And a linked worktree created from a tag:
detached, while the main working tree

130
00:10:04,776 --> 00:10:05,602
stays on main.

131
00:10:07,442 --> 00:10:09,090
Five mistakes to watch for.

132
00:10:09,430 --> 00:10:13,905
Commits "disappeared" after switching
branches. Root cause: they were made in

133
00:10:13,945 --> 00:10:18,713
detached HEAD and were reachable only
through HEAD; git reflog shows them, and git

134
00:10:18,753 --> 00:10:20,824
branch <name> <id> brings them back.

135
00:10:21,164 --> 00:10:24,859
Treating the detached-HEAD message
as an error to make go away.

136
00:10:24,899 --> 00:10:29,602
Root cause: it is a normal state that Git
itself uses for bisect and rebase; the

137
00:10:29,642 --> 00:10:30,669
message is advice.

138
00:10:31,009 --> 00:10:34,699
A script that calls git branch
--show-current fails in CI.

139
00:10:34,739 --> 00:10:39,973
Root cause: CI jobs, deploy checkouts and
submodules are in detached HEAD, and the

140
00:10:40,013 --> 00:10:41,839
command prints nothing there.

141
00:10:42,179 --> 00:10:46,322
git push with no refspec from a
deploy checkout does nothing useful.

142
00:10:46,362 --> 00:10:51,305
Root cause: no branch is current; use git
rev-parse HEAD for the commit, and name

143
00:10:51,345 --> 00:10:53,459
the branch explicitly when pushing.

144
00:10:53,799 --> 00:10:58,578
Assuming an interrupted rebase broke the
branch. Root cause: a rebase runs on a

145
00:10:58,618 --> 00:11:01,689
detached HEAD and moves the
branch only at the end.

146
00:11:03,529 --> 00:11:08,385
Now, out of the lab. The textbook states
the production rule in one paragraph.

147
00:11:08,425 --> 00:11:13,658
Detached HEAD is the normal state of a CI
job, a deploy checkout and a submodule, so

148
00:11:13,698 --> 00:11:17,989
a script that assumes a current
branch fails in exactly those places.

149
00:11:18,329 --> 00:11:23,545
An ML team's release job clones the
repository at a tag, generates a model card,

150
00:11:23,585 --> 00:11:27,355
commits it, and pushes. The
commit succeeds, onto nothing.

151
00:11:27,395 --> 00:11:32,676
The push has no refspec, meaning it names
no branch, so it has no branch to push.

152
00:11:32,716 --> 00:11:38,194
The repaired job uses git rev-parse HEAD to
record the commit it built from, creates

153
00:11:38,234 --> 00:11:43,515
a branch with git switch -c before committing,
and names that branch in the push.

154
00:11:45,355 --> 00:11:49,556
Your turn. Do Lab 4.2,
"Detached HEAD rescue", in

155
00:11:49,596 --> 00:11:52,758
lab-manual/m04-refs-branches-head.md.

156
00:11:53,098 --> 00:11:58,628
Before you leave the detached HEAD in the
lab, write down what you expect in three

157
00:11:58,668 --> 00:12:02,636
places afterwards: git log
--all, git reflog, and git fsck.

158
00:12:02,676 --> 00:12:04,607
Then switch away and compare.

159
00:12:06,447 --> 00:12:11,265
Q98: "A colleague's two days of commits
are "gone" after she checked out a tag and

160
00:12:11,305 --> 00:12:16,063
later switched back to main. Walk through
the recovery and explain why it works."

161
00:12:18,403 --> 00:12:23,197
Answer out loud. A strong answer starts
with the state of HEAD after the checkout,

162
00:12:23,237 --> 00:12:28,148
and says which ref her commits moved. It
names where the IDs can still be found, and

163
00:12:28,188 --> 00:12:33,100
the one command that makes them reachable.
It explains why the objects were never in

164
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:36,283
danger at that point, and
says what the time limit is.

165
00:12:36,323 --> 00:12:39,112
Mention what Git printed
when she switched away.

166
00:12:40,952 --> 00:12:46,509
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say: detached HEAD means .git/HEAD holds

167
00:12:46,549 --> 00:12:52,172
a commit ID instead of a branch name. It's
a normal state, entered by checking out a

168
00:12:52,212 --> 00:12:57,769
tag, a commit or a remote-tracking branch,
and by bisect, rebase, a clone at a tag,

169
00:12:57,809 --> 00:13:01,207
a worktree from a tag,
submodules and CI checkouts.

170
00:13:01,547 --> 00:13:06,446
Commits made there advance HEAD only. When
I switch away, Git names the commits and

171
00:13:06,486 --> 00:13:11,384
prints the command that keeps them. Afterwards
the HEAD reflog still has their IDs.

172
00:13:11,424 --> 00:13:14,299
Keeping the work is one
command: create a branch.

173
00:13:16,139 --> 00:13:18,876
Read section 7.7 of Chapter 7.

174
00:13:19,216 --> 00:13:26,028
Challenge: Exercise 4.4, Level 2, "a
commit made while HEAD was detached", in

175
00:13:26,068 --> 00:13:28,964
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md.

176
00:13:29,304 --> 00:13:34,310
A phrase that sounds like damage is now
a state you can enter, use and leave on

177
00:13:34,350 --> 00:13:39,484
purpose. Try the rescue in the lab yourself.
Next time: divergence, ancestry, and

178
00:13:39,524 --> 00:13:44,722
why Git has no parent-branch concept. Until
then, look at the state first and type

179
00:13:44,762 --> 00:13:46,766
second. See you in the next one.
